\section{What a row block reinterprets, and how that is handled}{

A limma row fitter reads three things off the shape of the matrix it is handed, so a
block can quietly answer a different question than the whole matrix would. All three
were measured against limma 3.62.2.

\code{asMatrixWeights} tests its gene branch (\code{lw == 1 || lw == dim[1]}) before its array
branch (\code{lw == dim[2]}), so a bare length-narrays weight vector is read as GENE weights
by any block whose row count happens to equal the array count. Splitting a 40 by 8
matrix into 8-row blocks moved coefficients by 0.325. Weights are therefore expanded to a
full matrix ONCE, against the full dimensions, and row-subset with limma's own
\code{arrayweights} attribute carried along.

\code{NoProbeWts} selects between two numerically different algorithms that also return
different component sets: the fast path returns \code{lm.fit}'s whole object including \code{qr}
and \code{assign}, the slow path a bare seven-element list without them. One NA cell in a 400
gene matrix put the whole matrix on the slow path and every block on the fast one, and
114 of 400 sigma differed. Splitting is refused, in favor of one plain original call,
unless every block provably lands on the same side as the full matrix.

\code{stats::lm.fit} demotes a one-column response with \code{if (is.matrix(y) && ny == 1L) y <- drop(y)}, and limma feeds it \code{t(M)}, so a one-gene block switches sigma from
\code{colMeans} to \code{mean} and loses the gene name: 4,080 of 16,000 one-gene blocks returned a
different sigma and all 2000 lost the name. Every split here holds at least two genes.
}

\section{Consensus correlation}{

\code{correlation} has no default in \code{lmFit} and the blocked branch tests
\code{missing(correlation)}, so missingness is forwarded rather than turned into \code{NULL}. An
explicit \code{correlation = NULL} does reach \code{gls.series}, which then calls
\code{duplicateCorrelation} itself, a trimmed mean over whatever genes it was given. Per
block that is a different consensus for every block. It is resolved once here, on the
full matrix, by \code{\link[=duplicateCorrelation_parallel]{duplicateCorrelation_parallel()}} with the same arguments and the backend's
own \code{duplicateCorrelation}, which returns \code{identical()} output, and the resulting scalar is
what the blocks see. When \code{\link[=duplicateCorrelation_parallel]{duplicateCorrelation_parallel()}} refuses to split that limma, the
backend's own \code{duplicateCorrelation} runs whole instead; any other error is raised as is.
}

\section{Dispatches too small to be worth a fork}{

The two branches of \code{lm.series} are not the same job. With probe weights, or an \code{EList}
from \code{\link[limma:voom]{limma::voom()}}, limma runs an R loop over genes, and that forks well: measured
3.92x unblocked and 4.69x blocked on 60,000 genes by 48 samples at the default 8 workers on
an M6 (\code{tools/bench_lmfit.R} in the GitHub repository,
\url{https://github.com/NamStacks/RNA-Parallel}),
against 0.60x to 0.69x at 2,000 genes with the gates forced open. This branch takes two gates:
\code{getOption("combat.min.cells", 20000)} cells, the gate ComBat-seq uses, and
\code{getOption("combat.min.wt.genes", 2000)} genes, because the loop's cost is per gene and it
is genes per worker that pay for a fork. A call below either one is one plain original call.

A matrix with a non-finite cell takes that same per-gene loop even without weights. When two
or more rows hold one, it is split so that every block holds such a row, under the two gates
above instead of the one below; a single such row is one plain original call.

Without probe weights limma fits every gene in one vectorized \code{lm.fit}, which costs
milliseconds and is mostly not worth handing to another process. That branch measured
0.24x at 1.2 million cells and 0.56x at 4.8 million, turning over to 1.47x at 12 million
and 1.71x at 20 million. It gets its own, much higher
\code{getOption("combat.min.ls.cells", 6e6)}; sharing one gate made it four times slower than
calling limma directly. Cells is only a proxy, since the cost rises with array count
too, so the gate sits at the smallest size measured to win.

On a backend that copies the payload to its workers (\code{"serial"}, \code{future} without a
multicore plan, \code{foreach} without a forking \code{doParallel} or \code{doMC} registration,
\code{options(combat.fork = FALSE)}, and every backend on Windows), an unset
\code{combat.min.cells} or \code{combat.min.ls.cells} closes entirely, so the call is one original
call there unless the option is set. To dispatch unconditionally, set \code{combat.min.cells}
and \code{combat.min.wt.genes} to 0 for the weighted branch and \code{combat.min.ls.cells} to 0 for
the unweighted one; output is identical either way.
}

\section{Configurations this refuses}{

\code{method = "robust"} goes to \code{mrlm}, and \code{ndups >= 2} makes \code{unwrapdups} reshape rows so
that a gene no longer occupies one row. Neither is split, and both stop with an error
rather than returning a serial result that looks parallel. Each is checked on the value the
backend will use: the one passed, or else the backend's own default.
}

\section{When this is worth reaching for}{

It depends entirely on which branch your call takes, and the two are not close.

With voom or probe weights limma runs an R loop over genes, which forks well but has a
floor, and the floor is counted in genes rather than cells. Below
\code{getOption("combat.min.wt.genes", 2000)} genes the companion is one plain original call, so
it runs at parity. At and above it the split runs. Measured on an M6 at the default worker
count with the gate forced open at 8, 24 and 48 arrays, the split ran at 0.60x to 0.69x at
2,000 genes, 0.99x to 1.13x at 4,000 and 1.49x to 1.63x at 8,000 (the memory guard ran 6 or 7
of the 8 workers at 4,000 x 48 and above), so between 2,000 and about 4,000 genes it is slower
than the original: by 13 to 16 ms per call at 2,000 genes, falling to nothing near 4,000. The
large-input figures are under "Dispatches too small to be worth a fork" above.

Without probe weights limma fits every gene in one vectorized \code{lm.fit}, which costs
milliseconds, so the companion declines to split until the matrix is very large and is
near parity until then: measured on an M6 at 20,000 genes by 50 samples, below that gate,
0.91x, one original call plus about 0.75 ms. That is the gate doing its job. With voom
weights below 2,000 genes it measured 0.92x to 0.96x, one original call plus about 0.5 ms.

Under either gate the companion is one plain original call plus about half a millisecond.
Nothing on a single call; worth avoiding in a loop over thousands of small fits, which is
the one case a per-call size gate cannot help with.
}

